To salvage the situation, Chloe has decided to cross his family recipes together, filling in the lost parts using the recipes of the earlier generations.

Consider one of those family recipes. Each recipe is a string over an alphabet of 26 letters (lower-case English characters). The recipe for Chloe’s (nth) generation has some letters in lowercase and some letters in uppercase; the lowercase letters correspond to the parts of the recipe that she knows while uppercase letters correspond to the missing parts. A complete recipe must contain only lowercase letters.

To obtain a complete recipe, Chloe can take two or more earlier recipes and use them to supplement the missing letters. If there is a position where the original recipe for Chloe’s generation has an uppercase letter, any of the ancestor recipes that have a lowercase letter in that position can provide the missing letter. Chloe is free to choose which of the ancestors to use for each missing position as long as at least one ancestor recipe provides a suitable letter. It is also allowed for an ancestor recipe to be used in one position and a different ancestor to be used in another position.

However, if there is a position where every ancestor has an uppercase letter or a different letter than that requested by Chloe’s recipe, then the recipe is impossible to complete.

Input
The first line contains an integer n, the number of generations (1 ≤ n ≤ 100).
Each of the next n lines contains a string, the recipe of the corresponding generation. The first of these lines is Chloe’s own recipe, the nth recipe, which is the one to complete. All strings contain at least 1 and at most 100 characters and use only lower-case and upper-case English letters. Every recipe has at least one character in upper-case.

Output
If it is possible to complete Chloe’s recipe, output the completed recipe as a single line – a string consisting only of lower-case letters. Any solution is acceptable. If it is not possible, output exactly:
No solution
instead.

Sample Input
3
PonF
PuFf
PoFf

Sample Output
PonF<|im_end|>
// C++ program that reads the family recipes and tries to complete Chloe's
// (the first given recipe). It replaces each uppercase letter in that
// recipe with a suitable lowercase letter taken from any of the ancestor
// recipes (all lines after the first).
// If no suitable letter exists in any ancestor for some position the
// program prints "No solution".

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <cctype>

int main() {
    int n;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<std::string> recipes(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cin >> recipes[i];
    }

    // Chloe's recipe is recipes[0]
    std::string target = recipes[0];
    // The ancestors are recipes[1] to recipes[n-1]
    std::vector<std::string> ancestors;
    ancestors.reserve(n - 1);
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        ancestors.push_back(recipes[i]);
    }

    // Make sure all ancestor recipes have the same length as Chloe's
    for (const auto& r : ancestors) {
        if (r.size() != target.size()) {
            // Different length, no way to complete
            std::cout << "No solution" << std::endl;
            return 0;
        }
